            <ArticleTitle>An unusual cause of peritonitis in a deployed environment.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>69-70</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/jramc-2013-000185</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Acute abdominal pain is a common presenting complaint to both primary and secondary care, and is a frequent cause of hospital admission among deployed personnel. Identification of generalised peritonism on abdominal examination is a classical indicator of intra-abdominal pathology that may warrant exploratory laparotomy. Negative findings at laparotomy should serve as a diagnostic prompt to consider other non-surgical mimics of an acute abdomen.</AbstractText>
